hey this is presh tallwalker  construct a semi-circle with center o  label one of its endpoints as a  and then construct a perpendicular ob  to ao  now connect a and b together  and let its midpoint be point d  from there let's construct a semi-circle  centered at point d  now consider  the area  between a e b f  and the triangle [ __ ]  what is the ratio between the yellow  area aevf and the blue triangle [ __ ]  this is a famous historical problem  aebf is called a loon deriving from the  latin word luna for moon  this is credited to hippocrates of kaios  who solved this in ancient times nearly  a hundred years before euclid  now i should mention this is not the  same hippocrates of course who's the  doctor of hippocratic oath fame  so you might think this is a very basic  geometric problem  but remember this was even before euclid  wrote the elements  this is said to be the first precise  mathematical calculation of an area  between curved lines  this is even before the familiar formula  for the area of a circle  so  hippocrates use very basic principles  this was solved using the pythagorean  theorem  and the second fact that the area of a  circle is proportional to its diameter  squared  using just these principles can you  solve for the ratio between the yellow  loon and the blue triangle  i want to thank sahil day and byron from  melbourne who suggested this problem can  you figure it out  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for a  historical solution  first let's label the diameter of the  circle as ac  since [ __ ] is an isosceles right triangle  a b is equal to ao times the square root  of 2 by the pythagorean theorem  we can then deduce that the diameter ac  which is equal to 2 times the radius ao  is equal to a b times the square root of  2.  because a circle's area is proportional  to its diameter squared  semicircle abc has two times the area as  semi-circle aeb  consequently half of semicircle abc  which is the quarter circle afboa  has the same area as the semicircle aeb  in other words  the area of this quarter circle  is exactly equal to the area of this  semi-circle  and now we've pretty much solved it  here comes the magical step the loon aeb  f and the triangle [ __ ] are both found by  subtracting the same area  afba  from shapes that have equal area the  semicircle aeb and the quarter circle  afboa  thus the loon and the triangle are found  by subtracting the same area from shapes  of equal area  therefore their areas are equal  you can also write this out  algebraically  and you will note that these two shapes  have the same area and you're  subtracting the same area from each of  them  therefore the two areas are equal  so i assume you'll be able to figure  this out because it's a basic  geometrical problem  but it should remind you some of these  problems that we find very basic and  routine today  were challenging thousands of years ago  so i hope this inspires you to solve a  problem today  with students thousands of years from  now will learn in grade school  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math you can catch me on my blog mind  your decisions if you like this video  you can check out my books which are  listed in the video description and you  can support me on patreon if you have a  math topic you can email me presh  mindyourdecisions.com and you can catch  me on social media either at mind your  decisions or at preshtalwalker
 

